## 2. PROTOCOL SPECIFICATION

### 2.1 Protocol Flow (vs x402)

```
x402 — 6 parties (client, server, wallet, facilitator, blockchain, RPC):
  Client → Server → 402 → Client signs → Facilitator verifies
  → Facilitator settles on-chain → Server delivers

AID — 3 parties (client, server, blockchain):
  Client → Server (request + AID DID + signed payment authorization)
  Server verifies AID offline (Ed25519, Merkle proof — ZERO network calls)
  Server verifies payment signature (ecrecover — pure math)
  Server submits EIP-3009 directly (or trust-defers settlement)
  Server → Client (resource + counter-signed receipt + attestation)
```

### 2.2 HTTP Headers

**DID Method Note:** The published spec and DIF submission MUST use `did:key` (W3C standard,
supports Ed25519 natively) as the primary DID method. `did:clawnet:` is the internal alias
used throughout this engineering document. Both resolve to the same agent via the same key
material. The migration from `did:clawnet:` to `did:key:` is complete — 34 references
updated (see BUILD PROGRESS CHECKLIST). Both resolve to the same agent via the same
Ed25519 key material. See Section 43.2 Flaw 11 for rationale.

**Crypto-Agility:** AID identity is algorithm-agile by design. The current reference
implementation uses Ed25519 (`did:key` with multicodec `0xed`) and SHA-256 for trust-layer
hashing. The protocol supports algorithm migration via versioned key types, enabling
transition to NIST post-quantum signatures (ML-DSA, FIPS 204) without protocol-level
changes. Hybrid Ed25519 + ML-DSA signatures are supported during the migration period.
All AID documents include `signatureAlgorithm`, `algorithmVersion`, and `hashAlgorithm`
fields. Verifiers MUST read these fields and dispatch accordingly — never hardcode Ed25519.
See `src/utils/crypto-agility.ts` for the implementation.

**Request Headers (Client → Server):**
```
