## 28. SCALING ARCHITECTURE (Verified Benchmarks)

### 28.1 What Scales Without Changes

| Component | At 10K agents | At 100K agents | At 1M agents | Bottleneck |
|-----------|--------------|----------------|--------------|------------|
| Ed25519 verification | 12/s needed, 5K-15K/s capacity | 120/s, fine | 1,200/s, fine | Never (1000x headroom) |
| Redis trust cache | 5MB | 50MB | 500MB | Never (25GB+ available) |
| Merkle tree build | <100ms | 200ms | 1-3s | At 10M+ leaves |
| On-chain anchoring | $7/year (1 tx/6h) | $7/year | $7/year | Never (always 1 tx) |
| SQLite reads | Sub-ms | Sub-ms | Sub-ms with indexes | Never for point lookups |

### 28.2 What Needs Changes at Scale

**Trust score computation (incremental update strategy):**
```
Brute force (recompute all): 1M agents × 5ms = 83 minutes ← too slow

Incremental (only agents with new activity):
  Query: SELECT DISTINCT owner_key FROM attestations WHERE created_at > ?
  Typically 5% of agents have new activity per 4h window
  1M × 5% = 50K agents × 5ms = 4 minutes ← fast enough

Materialized aggregates (best):
  On each new attestation → update running averages in agent_trust_cache table
  Trust cron just reads pre-computed aggregates → 1M agents in <1 minute
```

**SQLite capacity planning:**
```
< 50K agents (< 50M rows):    SQLite is perfect. No changes.
50K-100K agents (50-100M rows): Archive attestations older than 90 days to S3.
                                 Keep SQLite under 50M hot rows.
100K+ agents:                   Plan Postgres migration for trust/attestation data.
                                 Receipts already use tiered storage (Section 28.3).
```

**Receipt storage (tiered archival):**
```
Hot (0-7 days):    SQLite WAL — full query speed
Warm (7-90 days):  Separate SQLite archive file — attach on demand
Cold (90d-2yr):    S3-compatible storage, gzip compressed (5-10x)
                   Cost: ~$12/month per year of data at 1M receipts/day
Frozen (2yr+):     S3 Glacier — $2/month per year of data

Permanent index table in main SQLite:
  receipt_id, agent_did, created_at, sha256_hash, storage_tier
  ~100 bytes/row = 36.5GB/year for 1M receipts/day
  This index enables O(1) lookup of any receipt regardless of tier.

Monthly: compute Merkle root of all receipts → anchor on-chain ($0.001).
Any receipt can be verified against its monthly Merkle root forever.
```

### 28.3 On-Chain Trust Oracle — Merkle Root, Not Individual Writes

```
WRONG: Write 1M trust scores individually to Base every 6h
  Cost: 1M × $0.001 = $1,000/batch = $1.46M/year ← insane

RIGHT: Compute Merkle tree of all scores off-chain, write single root
  Cost: 1 tx × $0.001 = $0.001/batch = $1.46/year ← 1M× cheaper

  Verification: Anyone requests an agent's trust score + Merkle proof
  from ClawNet's API. They verify the proof against the on-chain root.
  Proof size: 20 hashes for 1M agents = 640 bytes. Always O(log n).

  For full transparency: publish the complete trust score dataset to IPFS
  alongside the Merkle root. Anyone can download and verify independently.
```

