### 41.4 SECURITY GAPS (3 Missing Threat Vectors)

These supplement the 33 attack vectors already documented in Sections 20 and 32.

**Attack Vector 34: MCP Server Supply Chain Compromise**

The @aidprotocol/mcp-trust middleware runs inside third-party MCP servers. A compromised MCP server
could intercept Ed25519 private keys from requests, forge trust verifications locally, or serve fake
trust data to callers. Section 39.1 addresses fail-closed for API outages but not malicious operators.

**Mitigation:** The middleware MUST never handle raw private keys — this is a security invariant.
It receives signed proofs and verifies them; the private key never leaves the agent. Add a "trust
chain" where the middleware's verification results are counter-signed by ClawNet's API, creating
two-party verification that a single compromised MCP server cannot fake.
Document this invariant in the SDK README and enforce it architecturally.

**Attack Vector 35: Temporal Trust Attacks (Coordinated Exit)**

An agent builds trust score 90+ over 6 months, then executes a coordinated attack in one hour:
use deferred settlement to consume $50 in services, simultaneously sell all bonding curve stakes,
and disappear. Burst detection (Section 26.3) catches spending spikes but not the coordinated
exit across multiple systems (staking + spending + identity).

**Mitigation:** Cross-system anomaly detection: if an agent's staking position drops >50% AND
spending spikes >3x average in the same 24h window, freeze all deferred settlement and require
immediate payment. Implementation: `checkCoordinatedExitRisk(did)` called on every deferred
settlement request, queries staking changes from last 24h.

**Attack Vector 36: Multi-Party Collusion Rings**

A provider and consumer collude to generate fake high-quality attestations to inflate trust scores.
Mutual feedback decay (Section 39.9) catches bilateral patterns but not multi-party rings
(A rates B positively, B rates C positively, C rates A positively).

**Mitigation:** Graph-based anomaly detection: build a feedback graph and flag clusters where all
edges are positive. If N agents form a fully-connected positive-feedback subgraph AND those
agents have limited interactions outside the cluster, flag for review and apply a cluster-wide
trust penalty. Implementation: periodic graph analysis in snapshot cron (every 4h), flag clusters
with >80% internal positive edges and <20% external interactions.

