### 41.6 OPTIMIZATIONS (6 Recommendations)

**Technical Optimization 1: Replace SQLite Before Phase 2**

The plan suggests Postgres migration at 100K+ agents (Section 28.2). But deferred settlement
with concurrent credit checks (Section 19.16) uses SQLite's exclusive locks. Under batched
settlement load, this serializes ALL deferred payment verifications. At 1,000 concurrent agents
with batched settlement, this becomes a bottleneck well before 100K.

**Recommendation:** Move to Postgres with row-level locking in Phase 2 (when direct settlement
ships). SQLite is fine for Phase 1 (credit-based, existing system). The migration is clean — the
SQL is already standard. Note: this applies to AID direct settlement only. ClawNet's existing
credit-based system on SQLite WAL is fine for current scale.

**Technical Optimization 2: Trust Score Caching Architecture**

Computing 11-dimension trust scores on every request is wasteful. Redis cache exists but
no cache invalidation strategy is specified.

**Recommendation:** Event-driven cache invalidation. Each new attestation triggers an incremental
trust score update (not full recomputation). Store intermediate aggregates (running averages for
each of the 11 dimensions) in Redis. Full recomputation only during the 4h snapshot cron. This
gives sub-millisecond trust lookups with near-real-time accuracy.

**Technical Optimization 3: Batch Settlement Gas Optimization**

Section 21.3 estimates ~$0.001/tx for individual receiveWithAuthorization calls. For batched
settlement, a single multicall contract can settle 100 agent batches in one transaction for ~$0.01
total (vs. $0.10 for 100 individual calls).

**Recommendation:** Deploy a BatchSettlement contract that accepts an array of (from, to, value,
nonce, signature) tuples and calls receiveWithAuthorization in a loop. 10x gas savings at scale.
A simple multicall pattern works today on Base.

**Strategic Optimization 4: Spec Document Splitting Is URGENT**

Section 40.7 item 10 recommends splitting into 4 documents. This should happen in Week 1,
not "eventually." The DIF submission requires a clean, focused protocol specification — not a
40-section internal strategy document. Submitting the full document to DIF would be
counterproductive. **ELEVATED TO WEEK 1.**

**Strategic Optimization 5: The Playground Should Be Day 1**

Section 27.1 describes a playground (no signup, interactive API explorer). This is filed under
"Adoption Accelerators" but should be the FIRST thing built. Every successful developer platform
(Stripe, Twilio, Algolia) launched with an interactive demo. The playground IS the sales pitch for
the DIF submission. Show, don't tell.

**Strategic Optimization 6: USDC Depeg Protection**

Section 37 uses a fixed CREDITS_PER_USD=1000. If USDC depegs (even briefly), this creates
arbitrage: buy credits cheap with depegged USDC, spend credits at full value (happened with
UST in 2022).

**Recommendation:** Query USDC/USD price feed (Chainlink or Redstone oracle on Base) and
apply a 1% slippage buffer. If USDC trades below $0.99, pause USDC-to-credit conversion until
peg restores. One free RPC call per conversion.

