## 42. PROGRESSIVE DECENTRALIZATION (The Bitcoin Question, Solved)

### 42.1 Why AID's Problem Is Easier Than Bitcoin's

Bitcoin had to solve: "How do we agree on which transactions happened in what order,
when anyone can lie, in real time?"

AID has to solve: "How do we agree on an agent's trust score, when the entity computing
it could lie or go offline?"

AID's version has three properties that make it fundamentally simpler:

| Property | Bitcoin | AID Trust |
|----------|---------|------------|
| Consensus speed | Seconds (double-spend is instant) | Hours (4h snapshots are fine) |
| Adversary model | Anyone can lie at any time | Lies are provable within 24h |
| Data privacy | Transactions need privacy | Attestations can be fully public |
| Computation | Simple (check sigs, sum values) | Moderate (11-dimension weighted formula) |
| Solution | Proof-of-work (expensive) | Deterministic computation + fraud proofs (cheap) |

**Three key properties of trust computation:**
1. **Deterministic** — given identical input data and the same formula, every computer
   produces the same output. No subjectivity.
2. **Staleness-tolerant** — a 4-hour-old trust score is fine for 99% of decisions. No need
   for sub-second consensus.
3. **Public inputs** — attestation data can be made public without harming anyone (unlike
   financial transactions which need privacy).

These mean AID doesn't need proof-of-work, doesn't need a blockchain, and doesn't need
miners. It needs something much simpler.

